---
import { site } from '../../config';
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'Why use an open-source website blocker?',
  description:
    'How source code makes browser extension permissions, storage, network requests, and release contents verifiable.',
  dateModified: '2026-08-25',
  proficiencyLevel: 'Beginner',
};
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Why use an open-source website blocker?"
  metaTitle="Open-source website blocker for Chrome"
  description="Learn what an open-source website blocker lets you verify: permissions, local storage, network requests, release contents, and blocking behavior."
  eyebrow="Technical guide"
  intro="Open source does not magically make an extension trustworthy. It does make important claims inspectable: what permissions are declared, where the list is stored, and whether anything is sent away."
  updated="August 25, 2026"
  structuredData={structuredData}
  toc={[
    { href: '#verify', label: 'What you can verify' },
    { href: '#limits', label: 'What source cannot prove alone' },
    { href: '#audit', label: 'A five-minute audit' },
    { href: '#architecture', label: 'How torwache is built' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="verify">
    <h2>Four privacy claims source code can support</h2>
    <dl class="decision-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Requested permissions</dt>
        <dd>The manifest lists the browser capabilities the extension asks Chrome to grant.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Network requests</dt>
        <dd>
          You can search for request clients, remote endpoints, telemetry libraries, and update
          services.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Storage location</dt>
        <dd>
          You can see whether the blocklist uses local storage, browser sync, or an external
          account.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Page access</dt>
        <dd>
          Content scripts and host permissions reveal whether code can run inside visited
          websites.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>A public repository is not the whole trust chain</h2>
    <p>
      Source availability does not automatically prove that a store package was built from that
      exact revision. Large projects may also be technically open while remaining difficult to
      audit because the shipped bundle is generated, minified, or dependent on remote services.
    </p>
    <p>
      Prefer small projects with reproducible packaging, readable release notes, few runtime
      dependencies, and a manifest that matches the store permission prompt.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="audit">
    <h2>A five-minute browser-extension audit</h2>
    <ol class="steps compact-steps">
      <li>
        <span>1</span><div><h3>Open the manifest</h3><p>
          Read the permissions and host permissions first.
        </p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>2</span><div><h3>Search for network APIs</h3><p>
          Look for <code>fetch</code>, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, telemetry, and remote URLs.
        </p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>3</span><div><h3>Find the storage calls</h3><p>
          Confirm whether data is stored locally, synchronized, or sent to a backend.
        </p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>4</span><div><h3>Check content scripts</h3><p>
          Understand which websites can run extension code and what that code reads.
        </p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>5</span><div><h3>Compare the package</h3><p>
          Look for a documented build or packaging command and inspect the release artifact.
        </p></div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture">
    <h2>Why torwache is intentionally easy to inspect</h2>
    <p>
      The source is strict TypeScript organized as small WXT entrypoints and modules for the
      blocklist, domain rules, storage, navigation guard, and blocked page. WXT and Vite compile
      it to browser-native JavaScript, HTML, and CSS; there is no runtime UI framework. The only
      runtime dependency is tldts, used to reject public suffixes such as <code>co.uk</code> and
      private suffixes such as <code>github.io</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can <a href={site.repositoryUrl}>read the repository</a>, inspect the typed
      <a href={`${site.repositoryUrl}/blob/main/wxt.config.ts`}>WXT configuration</a>, or
      <a href={site.releasesUrl}>download the tested release zip and its checksum</a>. The build
      generates the Manifest V3 file. Automated checks fail if its permissions or capabilities
      expand unexpectedly, and the package test verifies the ZIP's deterministic output and Store
      layout. The point is not “trust us because open source.” The point is that you do not have
      to.
    </p>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
